[38;5;231m⏺[39m One more structural thing, and it's a real constraint rather than a preference. [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m with a plain
  [38;5;153m<script src="app.js">[39m — no bundler, no modules. [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m uses CommonJS [38;5;153mrequire()[39m. So "one shared logger" has to
  physically get into two runtimes that currently have no shared module system between them.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how the logger file is consumed by both sides.

  - [1mESM everywhere:[0m convert [38;5;153mindex.html[39m to [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m, add [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m to [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m, and both sides
    [38;5;153mimport[39m the same file. Buys you one clean file and a modern baseline; costs converting [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m/[38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m off
    [38;5;153mrequire()[39m, and module scripts won't run from [38;5;153mfile://[39m — you'd need to serve the page over HTTP even for local
    testing.
  - [1mDual-mode single file:[0m one [38;5;153mlogger.js[39m that attaches to [38;5;153mwindow[39m when loaded as a script tag and exports via
    [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m when required. Buys you zero changes to any existing file and keeps [38;5;153mfile://[39m working; costs a small,
    slightly dated compatibility shim at the bottom of the file.
  - [1mShared core + two adapters:[0m a pure core module plus a browser wrapper and a Node wrapper. Buys the cleanest
    separation; costs three files where one would do, for an app this size.

  I recommend the dual-mode single file. It's the only one that adds logging without also making you adopt a module
  system and a local web server in the same change — and if you move to ESM later, the core logic moves unchanged.
